// ---------------- SCIENCE LESSONS ----------------
const LESSONS = [
 { id:"botany", n:"The botany of Camellia sinensis", body:[
   "Every true tea, from white to dark, comes from one evergreen species, Camellia sinensis, a relative of garden camellias. Two varieties dominate: var. sinensis, a small-leaved, cold-hardy shrub from China, and var. assamica, a large-leaved, more tropical plant first documented by Europeans in Assam.",
   "Left alone, tea grows into a tree; in gardens it is pruned to a waist-high 'plucking table'. The bud and first leaves are tender and rich in caffeine and amino acids, which is why the finest teas are picked as 'two leaves and a bud' or even buds alone.",
   "Herbal infusions such as rooibos, chamomile and mate come from entirely different plants, so botanically they are not tea at all." ],
   q:{ q:"What do green, oolong and black tea have in common?", o:["They come from the same plant species","They are all made from buds only","They are all fermented by microbes","They are grown only in China"], a:0, x:"The difference between tea categories lies in processing, not in the plant." } },
 { id:"oxidation", n:"What oxidation really means", body:[
   "Inside a fresh tea leaf, enzymes (polyphenol oxidase and peroxidase) are kept apart from polyphenols called catechins. Bruise, roll or crush the leaf and they meet: catechins oxidise and link into larger molecules, theaflavins and thearubigins, which turn leaves and liquor from green to amber to red-brown.",
   "Heat stops this. Steaming (Japan) or pan-firing (China) denatures the enzymes, a step called 'kill-green' or sha qing. Green tea is fixed almost immediately; oolong is bruised and fixed part-way; black tea is allowed to oxidise fully before drying.",
   "So the colour in your cup is a record of how long the leaf's own enzymes were allowed to work." ],
   q:{ q:"What stops oxidation in green tea?", o:["Heat that deactivates the enzymes","Adding salt","Freezing the leaves","Long sun-withering"], a:0, x:"Steaming or pan-firing denatures the enzymes before browning can begin." } },
 { id:"ferment", n:"Oxidation, fermentation and ageing", body:[
   "In the tea trade, black tea is often called 'fermented', but that is a misnomer: it is enzymatic oxidation, driven by the leaf's own enzymes. No microbes are needed.",
   "True microbial fermentation happens in dark teas (hei cha). In shou pu-erh, piles of moist leaf heat up as moulds, yeasts and bacteria transform it over weeks. In Fu Zhuan bricks, a specific fungus is cultivated on purpose.",
   "Ageing is slower still. Sheng pu-erh and pressed white teas change over years through gradual oxidation and some microbial activity. Yellow tea's 'smothering' is different again: a warm, damp, non-enzymatic yellowing after the enzymes have been killed." ],
   q:{ q:"Which tea is made by true microbial fermentation?", o:["Shou pu-erh","Assam","Sencha","Silver Needle"], a:0, x:"Wet piling (wo dui) relies on microbes. Assam is oxidised by the leaf's own enzymes." } },
 { id:"temptime", n:"Water temperature and steeping time", body:[
   "Different compounds dissolve at different rates. Amino acids like theanine (savoury sweetness) dissolve readily even in cool water. Catechins and caffeine, which bring bitterness and astringency, extract much faster as temperature rises.",
   "That is why delicate greens and gyokuro are brewed at 50–80 °C, while oolongs, blacks and dark teas want water near boiling to open rolled leaves and extract their oxidised compounds.",
   "Time, leaf size and ratio interact: broken CTC leaf extracts in two or three minutes, whole leaves more slowly. Gongfu brewing uses a lot of leaf with very short steeps; Western brewing uses little leaf with longer steeps. Cold brewing over several hours pulls out sweetness with little bitterness." ],
   q:{ q:"Why does cold-brewed green tea taste sweeter?", o:["Cool water extracts amino acids but far fewer catechins","Cold water adds sugar","Cold destroys caffeine","Cold water oxidises the leaf"], a:0, x:"Bitter catechins and caffeine are extracted much more slowly at low temperatures." } },
 { id:"caffeine", n:"Caffeine and L-theanine", body:[
   "Caffeine content depends more on the leaf than on the colour. Buds and young shoots, shaded tea and assamica plants tend to be higher; stems and late-season leaves lower. Brewing time, ratio and temperature change how much reaches your cup. The common idea that white tea is always low in caffeine is a myth: bud-only white teas can be quite high.",
   "L-theanine is an amino acid found almost only in tea. It tastes savoury-sweet and is concentrated in young, shaded leaves; sunlight converts it into catechins. That is why gyokuro and matcha taste so brothy.",
   "Because exact figures vary so much, the Atelier describes caffeine qualitatively rather than giving precise numbers." ],
   q:{ q:"Which statement about caffeine is most accurate?", o:["It depends on the leaf, cultivar and brewing more than on the tea's colour","White tea always has the least","Black tea always has the most","Green tea has none"], a:0, x:"Colour is a poor predictor; bud-heavy white teas can be strong." } },
 { id:"water", n:"Water hardness and minerals", body:[
   "A cup of tea is about 99% water, so the water matters. Hard water, high in calcium and magnesium bicarbonates, raises pH, dulls aroma, darkens the liquor and can leave a film on the surface.",
   "Very soft or distilled water goes the other way: it can taste flat and thin because a little mineral content helps carry flavour.",
   "Most tea drinkers find moderately soft, fresh water works best. A simple carbon filter jug often makes a noticeable difference in hard-water regions, and reboiling the same water repeatedly tends to make tea taste flatter." ],
   q:{ q:"What does very hard water usually do to tea?", o:["Dulls aroma and can leave a film","Makes it sweeter and clearer","Removes caffeine","Speeds oxidation of the leaves"], a:0, x:"Calcium and magnesium bicarbonates react with tea polyphenols and mute aroma." } },
 { id:"terroir", n:"Terroir, seasons and elevation", body:[
   "Where and when tea grows shapes its chemistry. Cool, misty, high gardens slow growth, which is generally linked to more amino acids and aromatic complexity; Ali Shan and high-grown Ceylon are classic examples.",
   "Seasons matter just as much. Spring 'first flush' shoots are tender and sweet; summer brings stronger sun, more catechins and, in Darjeeling, the muscatel second flush; autumn teas are mellow.",
   "Shade, soil, rainfall and even insects play a part. Oriental Beauty's honey notes start with leafhopper bites, a reminder that terroir includes the whole ecosystem." ],
   q:{ q:"High-mountain gardens are generally associated with…", o:["Slower growth and more amino acids","Faster growth and more bitterness","No caffeine","Fully oxidised leaf"], a:0, x:"Cool temperatures and mist slow growth and favour sweetness and aroma." } }
];

// ---------------- CULTURES ----------------
const CULTURES = [
 { id:"chanoyu", n:"Chanoyu — the Japanese way of tea", place:"Japan", body:[
   "Chanoyu ('hot water for tea') is a choreographed gathering in which a host prepares matcha for guests. Its aesthetic, emphasising simplicity and the beauty of the imperfect (wabi), was shaped in the 16th century by Sen no Rikyū, whose lineage continues in the Urasenke, Omotesenke and Mushakōjisenke schools.",
   "A formal gathering can include a meal and both thick (koicha) and thin (usucha) tea. Today many people meet chanoyu through short demonstrations or lessons, a modern adaptation of a practice once tied closely to samurai and merchant elites." ] },
 { id:"gongfu", n:"Gongfu cha — tea with skill", place:"China & Taiwan", body:[
   "Gongfu cha means making tea with effort or skill. It is associated with the Chaozhou region of Guangdong and southern Fujian, where small Yixing clay pots and many short infusions bring out the layers of oolongs such as Dan Cong.",
   "Its modern form, with gaiwans, fairness pitchers and tea trays, spread widely through Taiwan's tea-art (cha yi) movement from the 1970s and is now used for every kind of tea." ] },
 { id:"afternoon", n:"British afternoon tea", place:"Britain", body:[
   "Afternoon tea is commonly credited to Anna, Duchess of Bedford, in the 1840s, as a light meal to bridge the long gap before late dinners. It became a fashionable social ritual with sandwiches, scones and cakes.",
   "Everyday British tea is something else: strong black tea, usually from teabags, with milk. 'High tea' was historically the hearty evening meal of working families, not a grander version of afternoon tea." ] },
 { id:"atay", n:"Moroccan mint tea (atay)", place:"Morocco", body:[
   "Atay is Chinese gunpowder green tea brewed with fresh spearmint and plenty of sugar, poured from a height into small glasses to create a foam. Serving it is a central sign of hospitality.",
   "Tea arrived in Morocco through European trade and became widespread in the 19th century; Morocco is still one of the world's largest importers of Chinese green tea. Three glasses are traditionally offered, each tasting different as the brew develops." ] },
 { id:"pocha", n:"Tibetan butter tea (po cha)", place:"Tibet", body:[
   "Po cha is made by boiling dark brick tea for a long time, then churning it with salt and butter, traditionally yak butter, in a wooden cylinder. It is savoury, rich and warming at high altitude.",
   "Brick tea reached Tibet over centuries along the Tea Horse Road, traded from Yunnan and Sichuan for horses. Today many households use electric blenders in place of the traditional churn." ] },
 { id:"chai", n:"Indian chai", place:"India", body:[
   "Masala chai is black tea simmered with milk, sugar and spices such as cardamom, ginger and cloves, sold by chaiwallas across India in small glasses or clay cups (kulhads).",
   "Although tea plants grew wild in Assam, mass tea-drinking in India is relatively recent: it spread during the 20th century, helped by promotional campaigns, and CTC Assam became the standard base." ] }
];

